<ArticleLayout
  title="Stomach Flu vs. Influenza: What's the Difference?"
  description="Stomach flu and influenza are completely different illnesses despite the shared name. Learn the key differences in symptoms, causes, duration, and treatment."
  author="Dr. Sarah Mitchell, MD"
  reviewer="Dr. James Chen, PhD, MPH"
  publishDate="2026-07-29"
  lastReviewed="2026-07-29"
>
  <p>
    Many people use the term "stomach flu" to describe an illness with vomiting and diarrhea. But here's the truth: the <strong>"stomach flu" is not influenza at all</strong>. It's viral gastroenteritis — an infection of the intestines, not the respiratory system. Influenza (the real flu) is a respiratory illness that affects your nose, throat, and lungs. Using these terms interchangeably causes confusion and can lead people to misunderstand both conditions.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-2" class="citation">[2]</a>
  </p>

  <div class="callout-info">
    <strong>Key fact:</strong> The influenza vaccine does <em>not</em> protect against "stomach flu" (viral gastroenteritis). It only protects against respiratory influenza. If you got the flu shot and still developed vomiting and diarrhea, you likely had a different virus entirely.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a>
  </div>

  <h2>Quick Comparison: Stomach Flu vs. Influenza</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Feature</th>
          <th>Stomach Flu (Viral Gastroenteritis)</th>
          <th>Influenza (Respiratory Flu)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>What it actually is</strong></td>
          <td>Inflammation of the stomach and intestines</td>
          <td>Respiratory infection of nose, throat, and lungs</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Common causes</strong></td>
          <td>Norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus, astrovirus</td>
          <td>Influenza A and B viruses</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Primary symptoms</strong></td>
          <td>Vomiting, watery diarrhea, stomach cramps, nausea</td>
          <td>Fever, cough, sore throat, body aches, fatigue</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Respiratory symptoms</strong></td>
          <td>Rare or absent</td>
          <td>Prominent — cough, congestion, sore throat</td>
        </tr>
