  <h2>What Causes "Stomach Flu"?</h2>
  <p>
    Viral gastroenteritis is most commonly caused by <strong>norovirus</strong>, which is responsible for about 19–21 million cases in the US each year. Other causes include rotavirus (especially in children), adenovirus, and astrovirus.<a href="#ref-3" class="citation">[3]</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    Norovirus is famously contagious — it spreads easily in closed environments like cruise ships, schools, nursing homes, and daycare centers. It can survive on surfaces for days and resists many common disinfectants. Just a tiny amount of virus particles (as few as 18) can cause infection.<a href="#ref-3" class="citation">[3]</a>
  </p>

  <h2>What Causes Influenza?</h2>
  <p>
    Influenza is caused by influenza A and B viruses. These viruses infect the respiratory tract — the nose, throat, and lungs. They circulate seasonally, typically from fall through early spring in temperate regions. Unlike norovirus, which primarily causes gut symptoms, influenza viruses attack respiratory cells and trigger a systemic immune response that causes the fever, body aches, and fatigue associated with the flu.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Can the Flu Cause Vomiting and Diarrhea?</h2>
  <p>
    Yes, but it's <strong>much less common in adults</strong>. Gastrointestinal symptoms like vomiting and diarrhea occur in about 10–20% of adult flu cases. They are more common in children with influenza, where nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea may accompany respiratory symptoms.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-2" class="citation">[2]</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    The key difference: with influenza, GI symptoms are almost always secondary to respiratory symptoms. If your only symptoms are vomiting and diarrhea — with no cough, fever, or body aches — you almost certainly have viral gastroenteritis, not influenza.<a href="#ref-4" class="citation">[4]</a>
  </p>

  <div class="callout-warning">
    <strong>When to seek care for stomach flu:</strong> While most cases resolve within 1–3 days, dehydration is the primary danger. Seek medical attention if you or your child shows signs of severe dehydration: dry mouth, no urination for 8+ hours, sunken eyes, dizziness, or extreme weakness. Infants, older adults, and people with weakened immune systems are at highest risk.<a href="#ref-3" class="citation">[3]</a><a href="#ref-5" class="citation">[5]</a>
  </div>

  <h2>Treatment Differences</h2>
  <p>The treatment approaches are fundamentally different because the conditions affect different body systems:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stomach flu treatment:</strong> Focused on preventing dehydration. Oral rehydration solutions (like Pedialyte), small sips of water, and gradual reintroduction of bland foods (BRAT diet: bananas, rice, applesauce, toast). Antibiotics are useless — they target bacteria, not viruses. There are no antiviral medications for norovirus.<a href="#ref-3" class="citation">[3]</a><a href="#ref-5" class="citation">[5]</a></li>
    <li><strong>Influenza treatment:</strong> Antiviral medications (oseltamivir/Tamiflu, baloxavir/Xofluza) can shorten illness duration by 1–2 days if started within 48 hours of symptom onset. OTC medications address fever and body aches. Rest and hydration are essential. Antibiotics are not effective unless a secondary bacterial infection develops.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-6" class="citation">[6]</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Prevention Differences</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Preventing stomach flu (norovirus):</strong> Hand washing with soap and water (alcohol hand sanitizers are less effective against norovirus), thorough cleaning of contaminated surfaces with bleach-based cleaners, washing fruits and vegetables, cooking shellfish thoroughly, and staying home for 48 hours after symptoms resolve.<a href="#ref-3" class="citation">[3]</a></li>
    <li><strong>Preventing influenza:</strong> Annual flu vaccination, hand hygiene, avoiding close contact with sick individuals, covering coughs and sneezes, and staying home when ill.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a></li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout-info">
    <strong>Why the confusion?</strong> The term "stomach flu" likely originates from older medical literature that used "flu" more broadly for any rapidly-spreading acute illness. In some languages, "flu" translates to a general word for infection. Today, the medical community prefers the precise term "viral gastroenteritis" to avoid confusion with respiratory influenza.<a href="#ref-2" class="citation">[2]</a>
  </div>

